<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Epicurus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">341–270 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Epicurus was a Greek philosopher, born on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a> to Athenian settlers, who founded the Epicurean school. After teaching at Mytilene and Lampsacus he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> around 306 BCE and founded his community, the Garden. He taught that the universe consists of atoms and void, that the gods do not intervene in human affairs, and that the goal of life is tranquillity and freedom from pain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/epicurus.jpg" alt="Epicurus" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artistUnknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epikouros_BM_1843.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
